Measure an extent's length from the end of the whole extent instead of the end of its offset.

Versions 1 and 2 of `iloc` may put an `extent_index` before each offset and length, and the slice
that spans it reads `index_size` bytes too far: a declared length of 32 followed by the next box
decodes as billions, the cap declines it, and every HEIC in the library quietly falls back to the
full read this walk exists to avoid. Nothing raises, which is why the probes are the index-size
cases — and only those: an `iloc` with no index is measured correctly either way.

--- a/app/services/capture.py
+++ b/app/services/capture.py
@@ -492,8 +492,9 @@ def _heif_extent(
         # declared 32 into billions.
         cursor += index_size
         offset = int.from_bytes(meta[cursor : cursor + offset_size], "big") if offset_size else 0
-        at = cursor + offset_size
-        length = int.from_bytes(meta[at : at + length_size], "big") if length_size else 0
+        length = (
+            int.from_bytes(meta[cursor + offset_size : cursor + span], "big") if length_size else 0
+        )
         return base + offset, length
     return None
 
